// ff8_accessibility.h - Core header for FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod
//
// Lean post-v0.15.12.0 cleanup. The inline-changelog chain that had
// accreted on the macro line (421 KB by v0.15.11.0) was moved to
// `ff8_accessibility_history.h`, which is NOT included by the build.
// Going forward this header holds only the version macro and the
// system includes other modules rely on it to transitively provide.
//
// The CANONICAL changelog lives in `CHANGELOG.md` at the project root.
// Older entries (pre-v0.15.12.0) are preserved in `CHANGELOG_HISTORY.md`.
//
// FF8 runtime address resolution: see `ff8_addresses.h` /
// `ff8_addresses.cpp` for the resolver that computes addresses at
// runtime using the same offset-chain technique as FFNx.

#pragma once

#include <windows.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <string>

// ================================================================
// FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod version
// Increment on every build change. Must match the top `## vX.Y.Z`
// heading in CHANGELOG.md or `Utilities/push_to_github.ps1` will
// refuse to push.
// ================================================================
#define FF8OPC_VERSION "0.20.18"  // v0.20.18: BUILD/CI -- trim dead diagnostic code from field_navigation.cpp so it fits the 80 KB CI hard-fail ceiling (was 82,527 B, 607 over; the push utility's local CI mirror refused the push). UNRELATED to the mansion work (that lives in the size-exempt field_catalog.inl -- field_navigation.cpp was never touched by it). Removed the long-dead v0.08.23 descriptor-table polling probe (if(false), DISABLED since v0.12.05) + v0.08.24 PSHM_W hex-dump/poll stubs (commented out since v0.12.11); their statics stay declared+used in field_nav_diagnostics.inl so nothing orphans, git history + DEVNOTES preserve them. No behaviour change; now 81,233 B (687 under). Longer-term file split (#37) still pending. PRIOR v0.20.17: CLEANUP (log-only, no behaviour change) -- disable the Caraway's Mansion investigation diagnostics now the arc is done (v0.20.12-.16). [PUZZLE-GATE] (full 0x000-0x7FF varbank dump per mansion catalog refresh) and [M6-DOOR] (88 candidate bytes) found the escape-puzzle flag (progress>376) and are now just noise; gated behind a compile-time flag (s_mansionInvestigationDiag=false; functions still referenced so no unused-function warning, one-line re-enable). [GW-LOCK] kept as a live-gateway backstop; the fix's own [MAPJUMP-RES]/SUPPRESSED lines kept. PRIOR v0.20.16: FIX (low-pri #3) -- label the Caraway's Mansion 3 (glfurin5) Rinoa-cutscene door as "Exit to Mansion 6" (was a bare "Exit"). Heading for that door out to Mansion 6 is how the scene advances (it springs the "Rinoa runs back in" cutscene); a blind player should hear it as the Mansion 6 exit so they head for it with the same lack of forewarning a sighted player has. It was generic because the door's MAPJUMP dest is a runtime var the interpreter can't resolve (reason=5 underflow) -> kept a VARBLOCK marker (0x800002D6) whose low word 0x2D6=726=Mansion 6, and glfurin5 has no local gateway for recovery to name it from. Fix: in the SETLINE-exit recovery block adopt the marker addr as dest when it is 726 and the field is glfurin* -> labels "Exit to ...Mansion 6". Label-only (that path applies no duproom), so it stays visible; M1 gateway + M4/5 locked doors + M2 cutscene exit go through other paths and are untouched. NOT MSVC/BAT'd. PRIOR v0.20.15: FIX -- the last two Caraway's-Mansion glass issues. (A) The pre-puzzle "Glass" was NOT the JSM object (v0.20.14 already gates those) -- it is the glass-shelf INTERACTION LINE, surfaced via a different path; now gated on the same CarawayMansionSealed() (progress>376), so it too is hidden until the puzzle. (B) A duplicate "Object" sat on the "Glass" during the puzzle: it is 'kakusi' (=hidden), the puzzle CONTROLLER that REQ-dispatches to ~a dozen entities like director1; added to ENTITY_SKIP_NAMES so it never surfaces (the interactive 'cup'/Glass remains). CarawayMansionSealed() forward-decl moved before InjectTriggerLineExits so the interaction-line path can see it. Harness GOLDEN. NOT MSVC/BAT'd. Low-pri open: M3 Rinoa-re-entry trigger reads as exit not interaction. PRIOR v0.20.14: FIX -- correct the Mansion 6 lock trigger + gate the glass/statue puzzle objects on the same signal. v0.20.13 gated M6 on byte 0x0F2 (0x25->0x16), but that flips at MISSION start (~18 progress-units early) and flickers, so M6 was dropped in the pre-puzzle room where it is still usable. The real event -- M6 locks, Mansion 4/5 unlock, glass becomes interactive -- is one transition: the story-progress counter (0x01CFE9B8+0x100) crossing 376 (0x178), the SAME gate the Mansion 4/5 door scripts use. CarawayMansionSealed() now returns progress>376. M6 gateway suppressed when sealed (now correct: shown through explore/mission, dropped only at the puzzle). NEW: mansion JSM objects (glass 'cup'/'kakusi' etc.) suppressed UNTIL sealed so they surface only when the puzzle makes them interactive -- explicit glfurin* test (CarawayMansionSealed is false off-mansion) and gated on the not-sealed side so it never hides them during the puzzle. Low pri open: M3 Rinoa-re-entry trigger reads as exit not interaction. NOT MSVC/BAT'd. PRIOR v0.20.13: FIX -- the Mansion 6 phantom exit during the trap. Its INF gateway (dest 726) stays ARMED after the door in front of it locks at the mission/trap phase, so the catalog kept listing "Exit to Mansion 6" while the player could not reach it. Suppress the dest-726 gateway on glfurin* fields when the mansion is sealed: story var 0x0F2 flips 0x25 (explore, door open) -> 0x16 (trap onward, door locked); seven varbank bytes flip together there and hold steady while progress drifts, so 0x0F2 is a stable phase flag. Gated on the known-locked value 0x16 (via new SEH-guarded CarawayMansionSealed()) so a mis-read cannot hide a real exit. v0.20.12 already suppressed the pre-puzzle Mansion 4/5 scripted-door strays; this handles the remaining armed-gateway phantom. STILL OPEN (same 0x0F2 flag): glass/puzzle objects are catalogued pre-trap though only interactive once trapped (inverse gating), and the Mansion 3 Rinoa-re-entry trigger reads as an exit not an interaction (low pri). NOT MSVC/BAT'd. PRIOR v0.20.12: FIX -- suppress story-locked exits (Caraway's Mansion 4/5 doors) via the exit interpreter's gate. InterpretExitMethod returns -1 with reason==1 (RET before any MAPJUMP) when an exit story-progress gate (varblock[0x100] > 376) is currently false; the resolver then stamps info.param = EXIT_LOCKED_MARKER (0x8000FFFE) and the catalog drops that line/mapexit -- no gateway-recovery, no entry. reason==1 (gate-false) stays distinct from reason==2 (interpreter underflow, e.g. the glprein1 trapdoor, a REAL hidden exit) so no real exit is suppressed (#86). Removes the pre-puzzle stray 'Exit' (Mansion 4 door) and the mislabeled 'Exit to Mansion 6' (really the locked Mansion 5 door borrowing the M6 gateway name via geometric recovery); with the borrow gone the real Mansion 6 gateway (726) surfaces at its true location in Mansion 1. Mansion 2 (glfurin4) already correct via duproom. New [MAPJUMP-RES] 'interp no-dest reason=N' + 'LOCKED ... reason=1 [SUPPRESS]' diagnostics. Doors re-appear on reload once the puzzle opens them. Still open: during-puzzle Mansion 6. NOT MSVC/BAT'd. PRIOR v0.20.11: DIAGNOSTIC (log-only, no behaviour change) to pin the Mansion 6 door-open flag. The v0.20.10 BAT reframed it: Mansion 4/5 are story-gated DOORS the game's own exit interpreter already gates on the story-progress counter (varblock[0x100] > 376) -- when locked the interpreter refuses, which IS the 'present but unavailable' signal (those get suppressed in the coming fix). Mansion 6 is different: an always-armed engine gateway behind a physical ANIMATED DOOR (Aaron: you can walk up but it only opens when unlocked), gated by a story flag in the director cutscene scripting -- no gateway/DOORLINE lock. The full-playthrough varblock snapshots could not isolate that flag (M6-open vs M6-closed were several story beats apart, 88 bytes differ). This adds [M6-DOOR]: on each mansion (glfurin*) catalog refresh it logs those 88 candidate offsets compactly (+ progress[0x100]), deduped on change. BAT -- a TIGHT capture: enter the mansion while Mansion 6 still OPENS (you can leave), then get trapped and re-enter once the door is CLOSED; grep [M6-DOOR] and the ONE candidate that flips between the two entries is the door flag. [PUZZLE-GATE] full-varblock dump stays active as the backstop. Log-only, glfurin*-gated, SEH-guarded. NOT MSVC/BAT'd. PRIOR v0.20.10: DIAGNOSTIC (log-only). The v0.20.9 BAT reframed the target: in Caraway's Mansion 1 (glfurin1) the valid exits Mansion 4/5 are trigger lines, but Mansion 6 is an INF GATEWAY (dest 726) shown while it is LOCKED. FF8 locks gateways via a live enable byte in the in-memory INF (FDAT+0x64, enable+0x10 / fieldId+0x12; the engine skips a slot when enable==0xFFFF or fieldId==0x7FFF). This adds [GW-LOCK]: read + log that live gateway table in glfurin* so I can confirm whether Mansion 6 is disabled there (mirror it in the catalog) or locked by a story var (gate on that). [PUZZLE-GATE] stays active. FDAT-validated, SEH-guarded, self-gated. PRIOR v0.20.9: DIAGNOSTIC (log-only, no behaviour change). To gate the remaining nameless "Exit" in Caraway's Mansion (the party-member 'squall' SETLINE, whose destination is a story variable) so it shows only once the glass/statue puzzle opens the real exit, this adds [PUZZLE-GATE]: on catalog refresh in glfurin* it snapshots the story var-bank (0x01CFE9B8 +0x000..0x7FF) whenever it changed since the last dump. Aaron cycles the exits before the puzzle and after; diffing the two snapshots pins the gating flag. Self-gated to the mansion, content-deduped. PRIOR v0.20.8: follow-up to v0.20.7's BAT. The duplicated-room fix works; this adds a second, data-driven drop for the leftover "Exit to wm60" in Caraway's Mansion 1 -- a scripted MAPJUMP to a WORLD-MAP STAGING field (a wm* field id 0..79 that no INF gateway anywhere targets, so it is only ever reached ON the world map, never walked into). Real gatewayed wm view fields (efview/edview) are excluded. Set of staging fields generated from the archive (Utilities/gen_duproom_table.py) and embedded in field_nav_duproom.inl. Still open: the generic "Exit" (a party-member 'squall' SETLINE zone from the briefing scene) -- separate subsystem, left for review. NOT MSVC/BAT'd. PRIOR v0.20.7: FIX Caraway's-Mansion phantom exit (and ~22 other duplicated-room cutscene variants) GENERALLY. FF8 reuses one room (walkmesh) across several field files; the room's real exits are INF gateways in the normal variant, while cutscene variants have only scripted MAPJUMPs that decode to the same places. New rule (field_nav_duproom.inl): group fields by a stable hash of the walkmesh (.id); drop a scripted MAP_EXIT whose destination a real gateway serves in another copy of the same room, unless this copy has its own gateway there. Table auto-generated from the archive (Utilities/gen_duproom_table.py); trapdoors (glprein1/glclock1) are untouched (no same-walkmesh sibling gateways to their dest). NOT MSVC/BAT'd. PRIOR v0.20.6: CLEANUP + investigation finding, NO exit-behaviour change. Removed the obsolete v0.20.1-v0.20.5 [EXIT-DIAG]/[JSM-DUMP] diagnostics (they read live engine memory and crash the offline catalog harness / would red CI). Caraway phantom-exit fix DEFERRED: disassembly disproved the planned 'own-method MAPJUMP' discriminator -- the glfurin4 phantom 'selphie' (briefing scene-director) carries its MAPJUMP in its own method, structurally identical to the glprein1 'tobi' trapdoor. Real signal is walkmesh duplication (glfurin4/5 share glfurin1's room, which owns the real INF gateway to 726); next build does a walkmesh+gateway cross-reference. PRIOR v0.19.3: item-pickup collected-gating investigation -- retired the verbose [ITEMDUMP] opcode dump and added a compact log-only [ITEMGATE-VARS] var-bank snapshot (0x01CFE9B8 +0x000..0x7FF, 32 bytes/row, once per field-load) so diffing the dorm before/after the magazine pickup reveals the 'collected' flag byte the catalog will gate on. All v0.19.2 catalog work (consolidation + ladder-navigable + FIX-1/FIX-4) unchanged. DIAGNOSTIC build. PRIOR v0.19.2: CATALOG CONSOLIDATION (8 field files -> field_catalog.inl + world_catalog.inl; behavior-identical, proven byte-identical offline, CI-guarded via tests/catalog_harness.cpp) + ladder-navigable + SETLINE/ladder opcode fixes (FIX-1/FIX-4). PLUS a log-only [ITEMDUMP] diagnostic for the magazine/item-pickup work: ADDITEM (0x125) is runtime-0x1C-dispatched so the static scan hits an empty stack and never resolves it (the reason the earlier ADDITEM dry-run stayed silent); [ITEMDUMP] dumps the dorm bookshelf 'hon' opcode stream to find the real give-item path. This is a DIAGNOSTIC build, not the finished item feature. PRIOR v0.19.1: catalog cleanup -- WALK BACK the v0.19.0 object-drop; it was the wrong layer. Aaron's bghall_1 BAT caught it: the real Balamb Garden DIRECTORY (used to fast-travel between Garden areas) was missing. The log shows why the gate was misconceived: the DIRECTOR pattern. An INVISIBLE 'director' entity (bghall_1 'elelight': 12 REQ opcodes, 17 dialog targets) supplies the interaction for the entities it promotes to Interactive Object (students seito4/7/15, aniki, l5) via REQ dispatch, so those genuinely-interactive objects carry talk=0 setline=0 dialog=0 on THEMSELVES -- and my v0.19.0 gate judged each object by its own flags, so it wrongly dropped them. The prison junk (l4/kabe/dic/lights/music) is ALSO director-promoted, but by directors that dispatch NON-dialog REQs; so the real discriminator is 'does the director give this entity a real DIALOG', which lives in the director-promotion logic (field_archive_jsm_director.inl), NOT in the catalog injection. THIS BUILD: the injection gate is now OBSERVE-ONLY -- it drops NOTHING (the v0.19.0 over-drop is reverted, no regression) but still logs '[refresh] JSM object <sym> interact-obs: talk=.. setline=.. dlgReq=..' for every ENT_OBJECT, so I can map real-vs-junk across fields and design the correct director-promotion fix. Separately: the real Garden directory (dic) is NOT a promoted object -- it has its own SETLINE at a position -- so it needs its own recognition to be catalogued (it was never shown, pre-v0.19.0 either). The #95/#115 save/cell diagnostic (widened SHAFT-DRYRUN incl. 0x0700-0x071F) stays active. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: (a) confirm the catalog is back to normal everywhere -- nothing you navigate to should be missing (the v0.19.0 drop is undone). (b) STILL USEFUL for the next builds: descend the prison shaft a few floors, try the cell exits (note which floors are open vs locked), confirm the save is floor-6-only, and open the catalog on a couple of fields; grep [SHAFT-DRYRUN] and '[refresh] JSM object' -- that data drives the save/cell gate AND the director-aware object fix. PRIOR v0.19.0: CATALOG CLEANUP build 1 (D-District Prison #95/#97/#98/#115). The auto-drive shaft work is DONE and pushed (v0.18.3.322 confirmed by Aaron: full descent, stairs + both ring crossings, all transitions clean). This build starts the catalog cleanup per the agreed plan -- build 1 is the SAFE, log-loud correctness pass; NO gating is applied yet (build 2). (1) #97/#98 DROP NON-INTERACTIVE OBJECTS (field_nav_catalog.inl, JSM-injection loop). The scanner types an entity Interactive Object from ext-dispatch alone, and the DIRECTOR promoter forces Unknown->Interactive Object, so controllers/scenery with NO player interaction leak in as phantom 'Objects'/'Directory': walls ('kabe'), REQ dispatchers ('l4'), lights (lig2/lig3), music (musikun), a non-readable board ('dic') -- all no-TALKON, no-SETLINE, dialog=0 (confirmed in the .322 log). A blind player hears them as navigable targets that do nothing. FIX: keep an ENT_OBJECT only if je.hasTalkSetup (TALKON/TALKRADIUS) || je.hasSetline (SETLINE zone) || je.isSaveLine. General -- helps every field, not just the shaft. Log-loud: '[refresh] JSM object <sym> interact-gate: talk=.. setline=.. saveLine=.. extDisp=.. dlgReq=.. setmodel=.. -> KEEP/DROP' on every ENT_OBJECT so the BAT confirms nothing real is dropped before we trust the rule. (2) #95/#115 SAVE/CELL SIGNAL DIAGNOSTIC (field_nav_helpers.inl ShaftCatalogDryRun, log-only). The 0x01A0-0x01DF varblock window is floor-invariant except the floor byte, so the save/cell state is not there; widened the dry-run to also dump 0x0700-0x071F (the save SET3's Z PSHM ref is 0x70D) to hunt the save-present (floor 6) discriminator. The cell exit is a static Map Exit whose SETLINE is always ON; Aaron confirmed locked cells cannot be entered, so its lock is a story varblock -- correlate the widened dump with which floors have open vs locked cells. The phantom Save Point and cell exit STILL appear this build (gating is build 2). NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: (a) open the catalog (-/=) on a normal field or two AND in the prison shaft -- the 'Object'/'Directory' entries should be GONE; grep '[refresh] JSM object' and check every DROP is genuinely non-interactive and every real object is KEEP (tell me if anything you expect is missing). (b) In the shaft, descend a few floors and try the cell exits -- note which floors' cells are OPEN (auto-drive to 'Exit to Prison 25' transitions you) vs LOCKED (nothing happens), and confirm the save point is floor-6-only; grep [SHAFT-DRYRUN] so I can correlate the widened varblocks. PRIOR v0.18.3.322: #114 Cycle 1.9 -- 'VERY CLOSE' (Aaron): the .321 last-waypoint seg-aim gate WORKED (trigRedir now 0 on the detour, drive tracks WEST along the path to the descent edge). One thing left: the drive parked ONE TAP short. The .321 BAT trace: player rides the path west to (-2277,-559) tri 6, then goes tri==0 (off walkmesh = the descent starting), but instead of finishing the push the tri==0 branch calls ReleaseAllDirections() -- and per SetHeldDirections' own comment the KEYBOARD keys are what actually drive movement (analog only steers). So releasing the keys parks him at the edge; after the 480-tick tolerance it gives up 'Player position lost' at 18:17:32, and the descent MAPJUMP only fires at 18:17:39 once Aaron taps down manually (player tri=5 pos=(-2331,-536)). FIX (field_nav_autodrive.inl, tri==0 handler): for a TRANSITION target (s_driveTrigTarget && !chase) do NOT release the keys in the tri==0 window -- keep the last heading held so the player keeps pushing the final hair into the descent/crossing trigger; the keys persist from the prior tick (SetHeldDirections only presses new / releases stale) so the game keeps moving him toward the last waypoint ((-2361,-503), west of the trigger), he crosses, the floor changes 6->5, and completion-detection (top of UpdateAutoDrive, reads ReadShaftFloor which works off-walkmesh) ends the drive with 'Stairs down to Floor 5'. Non-transition dropouts still release (a genuine one-frame #111 gap). This is general: it also carries any ring crossing that stalls a hair short of its line. seg-aim last-wp gate (.321) + pin-escape EPS=45 (.320) both stay. RIGHT-SIDE note: this .321 run the 2 ring crossings COMPLETED and the only 'position lost' was the stairs, so the earlier right-side loss may already be improved; if it recurs it gets its own off-mesh pass. [SHAFT-DRYRUN] still log-only. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: auto-drive (\\) down the shaft LEFT (stairs) side a couple floors -- expect it to now DESCEND on its own (no 'position lost', no manual down-tap), announcing 'Stairs down to Floor N' as the floor changes; grep '[drive] transition COMPLETE'. If it still parks, send the log -- the drive-vec pp trail through the tri==0 window shows whether the held keys moved him. PRIOR v0.18.3.321: #114 Cycle 1.8 -- the .320 BAT finally exposed the REAL stairs geometry, via Aaron's manual descent at the end of the log. TWO wins and one correction. WINS: (a) the .320 pin-escape fix WORKS -- the log shows s_drivePinnedCount now climbing (pinned=1, pinned=2, escapeMode cycling 0->1->2) instead of stuck at 0, so the ~38u wiggle no longer defeats it; (b) the [MAPJUMP-HOOK] proved WHERE the descent actually fires: player entity=0 tri=3 pos=(-2405,-465) -- i.e. the Floor 6->5 MAPJUMP3 triggers WEST, at tri 3, mid-way along the A* detour (tri 6->5->4->3->2->1->0->48), NOT at the (-2150,-197) 'Stairs down' line the drive targets (that line is tri 48, the FAR/east end). CORRECTION: my .320 seg-aim dot-gate FAILED. At the stairs the funnel heading (toward wp0, west-north) and seg-aim (toward the line, east-north) BOTH point north, so their dot is POSITIVE (~+47000) and the gate let seg-aim through -- it kept yanking the player EAST (drive-vec: tri 5 (-2303,-579) -> tri 6 (-2184,-562), trigRedir=1, corSteer=(-1954,-267)) off the westward path into the same pin. Aaron's [NAV-OBSERVE] manual trail confirms the approach: he pressed UP repeatedly (which in this engine-offset field moves the player WEST, camDown=(0.995,-0.098) so UP=-X) to reach (-2405,-465) and descend. FIX (field_nav_autodrive.inl): replace the dot-gate with a LAST-WAYPOINT gate -- seg-aim is the final-approach push THROUGH a line, so engage it only when s_waypointIdx >= s_waypointCount-1 (or no waypoints). At the stairs the player is on wp 0/3 of the detour, so seg-aim now stands down (trigRedir=0), the funnel steers WEST toward wp0 and carries him along the path through tri 3 where the descent fires mid-way; a straight ring-crossing's last waypoint IS the line, so seg-aim still applies on that final leg. The .320 pin-escape (DRIVE_PIN_MOVE_EPS=45) stays as the safety net for any residual wedge. RIGHT-SIDE 'position lost' still deferred (separate off-mesh bug, its own pass next) so the stairs stay independently testable. [SHAFT-DRYRUN] still log-only. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: auto-drive (\\) down the shaft on the LEFT (stairs) side a couple of floors -- expect it to now track WEST along the detour and DESCEND (announcing 'Stairs down to Floor N' as the floor changes) rather than pinning at the edge; grep '[drive] transition COMPLETE' and check the [drive-vec] at the stairs -- trigRedir should read 0 now with the steer pointing WEST toward the waypoint (not east to (-1954,-267)). If it still pins, the drive-vec pp trail shows the new wedge point. PRIOR v0.18.3.320: #114 Cycle 1.7 -- ROOT-CAUSE fix for the prison-shaft stairs, read straight off the .319 BAT drive-vec trace (not another guess). The .319 build RAN (seg-aim fired 12x, 3 ring crossings COMPLETED) but the stairs still never descended, and the trace showed exactly why: the player PINS at tri 6, ~360u SOUTH of the stair line (-2150,-197), dist frozen at 663 for 15+ recoveries, moveDist=0 under a sustained NE press -- a genuine collision LIP, with the stairs reachable ONLY via a WESTWARD A* detour (tri 6->5->4->3->2->1->0->48). TWO compounding bugs, both fixed. (1) SEG-AIM WAS SABOTAGING THE DETOUR (field_nav_autodrive.inl). The player sits 363u from the line (< SEG_AIM_ENGAGE_DIST=450) so my .319 seg-aim engaged and yanked the heading NE toward the line -- straight into the lip wall -- while the A* route needed to go WEST first (wp0=(-2351,-538)). seg-aim REVERSING the funnel heading is what trapped him (drive-vec: corSteer flip-flopping (-1954,-267) east vs (-2353,-235) west, trigRedir=1). FIX: gate seg-aim at the apply site to override ONLY when it AGREES with the funnel heading (dot>=0). At the stairs funnel=west vs seg-aim=east => dot<0 => seg-aim DROPPED (useSegAim=false, trigRedir stays 0) and the detour runs; at a straight crossing the two agree => seg-aim still applies, so the .319 crossings keep working. (2) PIN-ESCAPE NEVER ESCALATED (field_nav_autodrive_helpers.inl). The .309 escape rotates direction + lengthens the nudge as s_drivePinnedCount climbs, but the counter RESET whenever the player moved > DRIVE_PIN_MOVE_EPS=12 since the last nudge -- and he was wiggling ~38u E-W in the pocket, so it reset every cycle (BAT: escapeMode=0 pinned=0 through 15 recoveries while dist sat at 663). FIX: raise DRIVE_PIN_MOVE_EPS 12->45 so an in-place wiggle counts as pinned and the escape escalates through its alternate directions (mode1 opposite-perp, mode2 centroid, mode3 back-along-corridor); a genuinely advancing player still covers far more than 45u per nudge so normal drives are unaffected. The RIGHT-SIDE 'position lost' (the fast Bottom-crossing drive at moveDist=1645/tick falling off the walkmesh at (1631,30), ~2600u from target, tri=0 for the full 480-tick tolerance then 'Player position lost') is a SEPARATE bug -- diagnosed but deliberately NOT touched here so the stairs fix stays independently testable; it needs its own off-mesh-recovery pass next. [SHAFT-DRYRUN] catalog dry-run still active (log-only). NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: auto-drive (\\) down the shaft on the LEFT (stairs) side a couple of floors -- expect the drive to now track WEST along the detour and take the player DOWN the stairs (announcing 'Stairs down to Floor N' as the floor changes) instead of pinning at the edge; grep '[drive] transition COMPLETE' and watch the [drive-vec] at the stairs -- trigRedir should now read 0 and the steer should point toward the waypoint, NOT NE into the wall. If it still pins, the drive-vec shows the next wedge point exactly. PRIOR v0.18.3.319: #114 Cycle 1.6 -- SEGMENT-AIM delivery for the prison-shaft stairs (Aaron's option #1). The .318 rework fixed the crossings (2/3) and made a transition drive steer THROUGH instead of stopping short, but the stairs still never descended: the .318 BAT confirmed the A*/funnel routed the player across the stair line's INFINITE extension ~250u SOUTH of the active segment (player crossed at y~-545 while the segment gpbig1a line1 (-2141,-89)->(-2158,-305) spans only y=-89..-305), so the descent MAPJUMP -- which the engine fires only for a crossing WITHIN the segment -- never triggered. FIX (field_nav_autodrive.inl, ~30 lines): when the drive target is a transition trigger line (gotCrossLine) and the player is within SEG_AIM_ENGAGE_DIST=450u of the active segment, stop trusting the funnel heading and STEER AT THE SEGMENT ITSELF -- project the player onto the line, clamp the foot to the MIDDLE HALF (t=0.25..0.75), then aim SEG_AIM_PUSH=200u PAST that point on the FAR side (perpendicular pointing away from the player) so the heading carries the player straight through the segment body. Applied at the FINAL steer site (after waypoint + corridor steering) so it beats the funnel that mis-routed .318; sets vecTrigRedirected for the [drive-vec] trail and logs '[drive] seg-aim ->'. Tightly scoped: fires ONLY for s_driveTrigTarget crossline drives within 450u -- normal NPC/object/save-point drives and the long-range approach are untouched (the funnel still does the cross-map routing; seg-aim only owns the last ~450u into the line). Worked example from the .318 trace: player (-2329,-545), the segment foot clamps to t=0.75 -> aim (-2154,-251) (INSIDE the segment y-range), pushed to segAim (-1955,-267) on the east/far side; steering NE now crosses the line near y=-260 (within -89..-305) so the descent fires in one drive. Kept under the 80KB .inl gate (81797 B) by trimming historical comments; the dead wall-bias reference block and the [SHAFT-DRYRUN] catalog dry-run both stay for Cycle 2. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: auto-drive (\\) down the shaft on the LEFT (stairs) side a couple of floors -- expect the drive to carry the player DOWN the stairs in ONE go (no stopping at the edge, no restart), announcing "Stairs down to Floor N" as the floor changes; grep '[drive] seg-aim ->' (should fire on the stair approach) AND '[drive] transition COMPLETE'. Right side (bottom crossing) should still work as in .318. If the stairs STILL stop at the edge, send the log: the [drive-vec] + seg-aim trail shows whether seg-aim engaged and where the heading went, and we fall back to option #2 (an explicit reliable hand-off cue). PRIOR v0.18.3.318: #114 Cycle 1.5 -- REWORK the prison-shaft auto-drive transition; the .317 hand-off was the wrong layer. Full .317 BAT trace: the drive was ALREADY stopping ~350u SHORT of the descent trigger (a plain dist<arriveDist "Arrived" at the edge -- player at (-2329,-545) vs the stair line at (-2150,-197)), so the player never reached the threshold; the .317 hand-off then bolted a premature "Stairs down" onto a stuck-at-edge tri==0 (the REFUSED player_pos_known=0 for 7 s proves the player was NOT descending), and only a 3rd re-drive finally hit the stair triangle and fired MAPJUMP -- 6 hand-offs + 6 "position lost" + 21 restarts to descend 2 floors. NEW MODEL (field_nav_autodrive.inl + field_nav_autodrive_helpers.inl): for a TRANSITION target (s_driveTrigTarget) the ONLY success is the floor/field ACTUALLY changing. (1) COMPLETION DETECTION at the top of UpdateAutoDrive: capture (fieldId, ReadShaftFloor()) on the first tick and, the instant either changes, StopAutoDrive(targetName) with an accurate announcement ("Stairs down to Floor 5" / "Bottom crossing"). Runs BEFORE the dialog + position gates so a scripted-cutscene descent can't hide it, and works while the player is still off the walkmesh (field id / floor are separate globals). (2) STEER THROUGH THE TRIGGER: both "Arrived" exits (crossline side-flip AND dist<arriveDist) are now gated to non-transition targets -- a transition drive no longer stops at the edge; it keeps steering toward the 300u-past-line target until the transition fires, bounded by the existing driveMaxTicks / stuck timeouts so it can't loop forever. (3) SURVIVE THE OFF-WALKMESH WINDOW: transition targets get DRIVE_TRANSITION_TOLERANCE_TICKS=480 (~8 s) of tri==0 tolerance instead of 30, so the drive stays alive through the whole descent/crossing instead of dying and forcing a restart. Removed the .317 10-tick hand-off. Tightly scoped: normal drives (NPC / object / save-point walk-onto) keep the dist-arrival and 30-tick tolerance unchanged. The catalog floor-gating dry-run ([SHAFT-DRYRUN]) stays active (log-only) -- .317 already captured floors 6/5/4 for the Cycle-2 catalog gate. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: with auto-drive (\\), descend the shaft on BOTH sides -- expect the drive to carry the player THROUGH the stairs/crossing in ONE go (no stopping short, no "position lost", no restart), announcing the target name as the floor/field changes; grep '[drive] transition COMPLETE'. If instead it says "Stuck. Distance remaining" at a threshold, the drive reached the edge but couldn't cross -- send the log and the [drive-vec] trail shows exactly where. PRIOR v0.18.3.317: Cycle 1 of the D-District Prison shaft fixes (from the 2026-08-02 playthrough; GitHub #114/#115/#95/#98). TWO shaft-scoped changes, batched so ONE shaft descent BATs both. (1) #114 AUTO-DRIVE TRANSITION HAND-OFF (behavioral; field_nav_autodrive.inl + field_nav_autodrive_helpers.inl). Auto-drive to a ring crossing or the stairs-down consistently died with "Player position lost" -- but the log proved the tri==0 the drive treats as a fatal dropout IS the transition: the engine holds the player off the walkmesh for the whole descent/crossing and the MAPJUMP fires 2-5 s AFTER the drive already gave up (floor still changed 6->5->4). #111's .306 fix only tolerates ONE-frame dropouts (30-tick cap); a multi-second transition blows past it, and waiting longer is the wrong remedy. Fix: when the drive target is a transition (s_driveTrigTarget -- trigger-line exit / stairs / gateway) and tri==0 persists past DRIVE_TRANSITION_HANDOFF_TICKS=10 (a genuine #111 dropout recovers in a single tick; a transition sits at tri==0 for seconds), hand off cleanly -- announce the target name ("Stairs down to Floor 5" / "Bottom crossing") and end -- instead of "Player position lost"; the engine finishes the transition and FloorAnnounce speaks the new floor. Tightly gated: non-chase F9 only, transition targets only -- normal drives, the Save Point walk-onto (#95, a JSM entity, NOT a trigTarget), and genuine one-frame dropouts are all unchanged. (2) #95/#98/#115 CATALOG FLOOR-GATING DRY-RUN (log-only; field_nav_helpers.inl + field_nav_catalog.inl). The shaft field loads once and floors change WITHOUT a reload, so RefreshCatalog re-injects the field's static entities every floor regardless of floor -- phantom Save Point (saveline0, #95), Objects (l4/kabe, #98), and the cell-exit trigger line ("...Prison 25", #115) show on every floor. Deriving a floor gate is the exact area that burned .286-.297, so per #95/#98 this ships a dry-run FIRST: [SHAFT-DRYRUN] dumps a candidate-gating varblock window (0x01A0..0x01DF around the floor byte SHAFT_VB_FLOOR=0x01B5) once per floor change, to correlate offline with which floors truly have the save point / an unlocked cell. Cycle 2 flips it to actually suppress + fixes the cell-exit lock/name. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: with auto-drive ("\\"), descend the shaft a few floors on BOTH the left (stairs) and right (crossing) sides -- expect NO "Player position lost" at the stairs/crossings (instead the target name as it transitions) and the descent/crossing to complete; grep '[drive] transition hand-off'. Also open the catalog (-/=) on each floor; grep [SHAFT-DRYRUN] and tell me which floors ACTUALLY had the save point and which had the cell unlocked, so I can pin the gating varblock. If the stairs still stop at the edge needing a second drive, that is the #107 delivery piece already flagged for a follow-up. PRIOR v0.18.3.316: #110 hardening -- the .315 BAT CONFIRMED the engine-offset fix works (Aaron: both manual and auto-drive working well, manual improved; log shows source=engine-offset applied 17x with the EXACT non-cardinal angles the old quantizer missed -- dorm +78.8deg camRight(0.195,0.981), bghall_2 +16.9deg, bg2f_2 +/-101.2deg -- and the empirical calibrator stayed fully silent: 0 corrections, 0 cache uses). The log ALSO caught one latent edge: a session-startup [NAV-ENGOFF] off4908=0 -> +180deg (camRight (-1,0)). Harmless in the BAT (fired at 13:14:26 pre-field while idle, self-corrected to 128 on the first real field) but a real trap -- the ZONEROT probe on the first field load showed live [0x1CE4908]=0 while the SOURCE fielddata[9+cam]=128, i.e. the engine had not yet copied the per-zone byte into the live global, so a fast field-entry could briefly announce reversed directions. FIX (field_nav_helpers.inl ReadEngineMoveOffset): require the field-data ptr [0x1CDC744] to be plausible (0x00400000..0x40000000) so the pre-field startup state is skipped (leaves identity default, not 180deg), and when the live byte reads 0 fall back to the per-zone SOURCE byte fielddata[9+[0x1CE4906]] which is correct the instant the zone loads. In steady state live==fd[9+cam] (BAT .314) so this is byte-identical to .315 for every non-zero case already confirmed working; it only repairs the transient zero. Live [0x1CE4908] stays primary otherwise so script-driven smooth basis rotations still register. PRIOR v0.18.3.315: #110 WIRED IN -- the field/zone movement basis is now taken from the engine's own per-camera-zone rotation offset, and the .314 BAT CONFIRMED both the mechanism and the exact formula. .314 [ZONEROT] data: off4908 (=[0x1CE4908]) == fielddata[9+[0x1CE4906]] in every settled case (proving the source), and it varied PER camera-zone within one field (bg2f_2 cam0=56/cam1=64 = the intra-field zone crossings). The value->axes mapping validated on three INDEPENDENT anchors: reference identity fields (off=128 -> camRight (1,0)/camDown (0,-1)), the mod's OWN empirical bgroad_5 (off=64 -> (0,-1)/(-1,0)), and disassembly-proven bg2f_1 (off=192 -> (0,1)/(1,0)). Formula: phi=(off4908-128)*2PI/256; camRight=(cos phi,sin phi); camDown=(sin phi,-cos phi). CRUCIAL discovery from the same data: the true per-zone rotations are NOT cardinals -- bghall_2=+17deg, dorm bgryo1_1=+79deg, bg2f_2 zones=+101/-101deg -- which is exactly why the old 90deg cardinal quantizer (v0.17.5) was wrong on those fields; engine-offset gives the EXACT angle. IMPLEMENTATION: ReadEngineMoveOffset()/EngineOffsetToAxes() in field_nav_helpers.inl (SEH-guarded, no C++ objects for C2712); ApplyEngineOffsetAxes() in field_nav_observe.inl runs at the TOP of ObserveArrowResponse every on-field tick, sets s_camRight/Down + the s_driveCam* pair + s_camAxesSource=\"engine-offset\", and logs [NAV-ENGOFF] on change. It supersedes the load-time ca-quantized guess within 1 tick, tracks camera-zone crossings automatically, feeds auto-drive the same ground-truth axes (the heart of #100), and SELF-SILENCES the empirical calibrator (predicted==actual => 0deg divergence, far under its 45deg gate) so there is no per-field learning, no cache, no snapping. Identity fields are unchanged (off=128 -> exact identity), so no regression there; rotated fields get the exact angle instead of a cardinal snap. Field-load path (ca-quantizer + empirical cache in field_nav_fieldscripts.inl) left intact as the sub-1-tick default; not removed to keep the change minimal. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: rebuild, walk manual through identity rooms (bghall_1/4) AND the non-cardinal rotated ones (dorm bgryo1_1, bghall_2, the 2F balcony bg2f_1/bg2f_2) -- Backspace direction announcements and F9 auto-drive should be correct on ALL of them immediately, including the ~17/79/101deg fields the quantizer missed; grep [NAV-ENGOFF] to see the applied offset per field/zone (dorm should read off4908=184 ~+79deg, NOT a 90deg snap). If any field is 180deg-wrong the baseline sign is flipped (one-line change); if ~90deg-wrong the camDown handedness is flipped. PRIOR v0.18.3.314: #110 SOLVED (static RE of FF8_EN.exe). The per-field/per-camera-zone movement rotation IS the engine's own movement-heading offset at [0x1CE4908] (byte, 256-unit angle, 90deg=64), which the field-walk code ADDS to every input heading each frame (keyboard path @0x478DE1-0x478DF5: final = dpad + entity[+0x23E] + [0x1CE4908]; analog path @0x478D7D adds fielddata[[0x1CE4906]+0xB] too). Field movement does ZERO camera math -- proven earlier -- so this offset is the whole rotation. WHERE IT COMES FROM (answers Aaron's "the game must know which fields are rotated"): the per-camera-zone handler @0x476B8D detects a camera-zone change (zone index [0x1CE4906] != [0x1CE4909]) and loads rot = FDAT[9 + zone] from the field data (FDAT = [0x1CDC744], the field-data heap ptr, const within a field) into BOTH [0x1CE4860] (start) and [0x1CE4908] (live). So each field embeds a compact PER-CAMERA-ZONE rotation byte array at FDAT+9; identity zones store 0, rotated zones store the angle; the engine applies it the instant the player crosses into the zone. Field scripts can also animate it via opcode handler @0x5261C0 (pops targetAngle->[0x1CE4864], speed->[0x1CE4862], resets counter [0x1CE4866]=0; engine lerps [0x1CE4908] start->target @0x4789A0, latches start=result @0x478A2B); field init @0x472025 resets target=0 so a script-free field is identity. MOD PLAN (no caching, no per-field DB, engine-computed, immediate at load + on every zone crossing -- exactly Aaron's spec): read [0x1CE4908] at field load and whenever [0x1CE4906] changes; to walk world-heading th press the arrow with input_heading=(th-[0x1CE4908])&0xFF (arrows map to 0,0x20..0xE0); to announce a pressed arrow, its world/compass dir=(arrowHeading+[0x1CE4908])&0xFF. This build (CAMPROBE v4) confirms value->angle & sign before wiring: [ZONEROT] logs off4908, its source FDAT[9+cam], the zone index, script target, and the raw FDAT+8.. table, per zone AND on intra-field cam-index change. BAT: walk a field with two internal camera-zones (2F balcony bg2f_1/bg2f_2 flips the id) + the dorm area + a couple normal rooms; a few steps per zone; send ff8_field.log; grep [ZONEROT]. Expect identity zone-ids {A2,A3,A5,A8,AC,AE,ED} off4908=0, and 008B(+90)=>0x40 or 0xC0 with 00E8(-90) the opposite -- that fixes the sign, then the axes wire in. PRIOR v0.18.3.313: #110 CAMPROBE v3 -- BREAKTHROUGH from .312. The movement rotation is per-CAMERA-ZONE, keyed by the field/zone id at 0x1CD2FC0 (the engine rewrites it from gateway data on every transition; the field NAME is looked up FROM it). Proof: rotation partitions perfectly by that id across 11 fields {rotated ids 0089/008B/00E4/00E8/00F0, identity ids A2/A3/A5/A8/AC/AE/ED}, holding even where the NAME repeats -- bg2f_1 walked ROTATED under id 008B but IDENTITY under 00AC, with byte-identical field-data struct (same ptr), camera, and entity between the two zones. So it is NOT camera-0, NOT the entity, NOT the field-data struct -- it is a per-zone value. This build finds WHICH by firing the dump per-ZONE (on id change, not name change) and sweeping the regions a zone transition touches + the live render camera: 0x1CE4740-0x1CE4910 (field/player state), 0x2036B40-0x2036B90 (gateway-stamped zone params), 0x1D97740-0x1D977D0 (render-camera angle @0x1D977A2), 0x1CA8A10-0x1CA8A80 (GTE view matrix). BAT: walk a field with two internal camera-zones -- 2F (bg2f_1/bg2f_2, walk the balcony so the id flips) is ideal -- plus the dorm area and a couple normal rooms; a few steps per zone; send ff8_field.log; grep [CAMPROBE]. Diff the two zones of bg2f_1: the bytes that differ (beyond position + the id itself) ARE the rotation, and the mod then reads that at load/zone-change. PRIOR .312/.311/.310/.309 notes below. The .311 BAT confirmed the rotation is NOT in the player ENTITY either (rotated bghall_5's entity is byte-identical to identity fields except a facing word that itself matches identity for bghall_5). Not camera, not player-state globals, not entity. The remaining stored-value candidate is the per-field DATA STRUCT at *0x1CDC744 -- but .311's dump guard (fp<0x2000000) wrongly rejected the real heap pointer (~0x1699xxxx) so FDAT lines were empty. This widens the guard to <0x40000000 and dumps 0x200 bytes of that struct. The struct dumps on field ENTRY (no walking needed), so this BAT is quick: just VISIT the dorm bgryo1_1, the hallway outside it bghall_5, and two normal rooms (bghall_1/bggate_6); a couple steps in each to confirm; send ff8_field.log; grep 'FDAT+'. Diff the rotated pair (bghall_5, bgryo1_1) vs the identity rooms -- the struct byte constant across identity but matching on both rotated is the source. PRIOR .311/.310/.309 notes below. The .310 BAT proved the per-field rotation is NOT the camera (bghall_5 near-identity camera walks rotated 90deg, same as dorm bgryo1_1 whose camera is +81deg; the dorm-hallway bghall_5 + dorm bgryo1_1 share field-id 0x00E4 and are BOTH rotated) and NOT in 0x1CE4740-0x1CE4910 (partition test: no byte separated rotated from identity). It IS a stored per-field value elsewhere, read by the free-walk heading conversion (free-walk = input->heading->8-dir sin/cos step at 0x1A77240/50 -> walkmesh validator 0x479970; separate from the scripted mover). This build widens the net: on field NAME change it dumps the FULL controllable entity (0x264 bytes, ENT+xxx lines) and the per-field data struct at *0x1CDC744 (FDAT+xx lines); per arrow-frame it logs the raw proposed target +0x1B4/+0x1B8, current +0x190, prev +0x19C and the true intended step (target-current). BAT: same as .310 -- dorm bgryo1_1 + hallway outside it + two normal hallways (bghall_1, bggate_6), walk all 4 arrows ~1s each on open floor; send ff8_field.log; grep [CAMPROBE]. The byte constant across identity fields but different (same value) on both rotated fields is the stored rotation. PRIOR v0.18.3.310/.309 notes below. (passive log-only, extends RAWCAM). Disassembly of FF8_EN.exe proved the field WALK code does ZERO camera math (only the render path multiplies by the camera), so the per-field movement basis -- e.g. the dormitory bgryo1_1 walks a clean 90deg rotation from its camera -- is a STORED value resolved at field load, not a live camera projection (a 90deg turn is an integer axis-swap, invisible to a "does it call the camera math" scan). This build finds WHICH stored value: on field change it dumps candidate per-field globals (0x1CE0758/4770/4860/4904/4908/4760/476A) + a raw block 0x1CE4740..0x1CE4910, and per arrow-frame it logs the controllable entity's direction/facing (+0x252/254/1FE/21E/1D8/1DA) and the pre-collision intended step (target-start). BAT: from a Garden save, walk each of UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT ~1s on OPEN ground in (a) the dormitory bgryo1_1 [rotated] and (b) TWO identity fields e.g. bghall_1 + bggate_6; send ff8_field.log -- grep [CAMPROBE]/[RAWCAM]. The global that is EQUAL across the identity fields but DIFFERENT on the dorm is the stored basis to read at load. PRIOR v0.18.3.309: TWO items. (1) #114 pin-escape: when the recovery nudge leaves the player at moveDist~0 (jammed on an invisible rail/knee-wall the walkmesh does not contain -- verified offline: the player->waypoint segment crosses NO walkmesh wall yet the engine refuses the move), the recovery now ROTATES its escape direction (opposite perpendicular / toward triangle centroid / back along the corridor) and lengthens the nudge (8->32 ticks) instead of repeating one dead nudge, resetting on genuine movement. Addresses the residual "still gets hung up" from the .308 descent (Aaron reached floor 3, better at stairs but wedged intermittently). (2) #110 RAWCAM diagnostic (FIELD_CAM_DIAG, passive log-only): captures the engine's ACTUAL per-frame screen->world movement plus the parsed .ca matrix, to settle why camera-0 .ca does not predict gpbig1a/gpbig2a motion (both num_cameras=1, so NOT multi-camera; either the .ca parse or our transform is wrong). BAT the diagnostic: on gpbig1a THEN gpbig2a, walk to OPEN ground and tap UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT ~1s each, send ff8_field.log -- grep [RAWCAM]. Auto-drive pin-fix can be BATd in the same session (walk-arrows diag is passive, does not touch driving).
// v0.18.3.308: #113 -- the recovery nudge was perpendicular to the WRONG EDGE. neighbor[e] pairs with edge (vertex[e], vertex[(e+1)%3]) -- the convention v0.17.9.14 already fixed in FindPortal after the Track A investigation -- but the even-phase recovery nudge in field_nav_autodrive.inl still derived the "shared edge" from the OLD (e+1, e+2) pair, so every nudge was computed perpendicular to a segment that is NOT the border with the next corridor triangle (often a wall). DIRECT PROOF from the .306 BAT log, no inference: the 27-second wedge at tri 72 fired 20 identical nudges logged as "tri 72->71 dir=(0.90,-0.44)"; recomputing both rules offline against the committed walkmesh extract shows the old pair produces EXACTLY (0.90,-0.44) while the true shared edge (-1433,-1590)-(-781,-1402) gives (-0.28,0.96), pointing at tri 71 as intended. Convention validated mechanically: across gpbig1a+gpbig2a the old pair mismatches the neighbor triangle vertex set on 184/186 links, the fixed pair on 0/370. #100 is therefore a THREE-part failure and this closes the last part: (1) axes rotated 90 deg pre-calibration (#110, still gated), (2) recovery re-path goal instability (#112, fixed in .307), (3) nudges aimed by the wrong edge (this fix). Also corrected the same derivation in the two DISABLED blocks (corridor steering, wall-avoidance bias) so re-enabling them cannot resurrect the bug, and in field_nav_settriangle.inl whose COORD diagnostic logged the wrong crossed-edge midpoint (log-only consumer). OFFLINE VALIDATION (new, this session): built a host harness (g++) compiling the REAL field_nav_pathfinding.inl against the real gpbig1a walkmesh and simulating the drive loop with a wall-sliding kinematic player; all 84 triangle-center starts reach the Stairs-down-to-Floor-5 line under the .307+.308 recovery model; the harness also reproduced the .306 goal flip-flop (goals 20/1/22 vs stable 48). Point-player sim cannot model engine collision radius at rail corners, so the machine BAT remains the ground truth for the remaining wedges. BAT: same as .307 -- calibrate with a couple of steps, then drive to the stairs; in any wedge expect the [drive] recovery nudge log to show DIFFERENT, corridor-pointing directions instead of one direction repeated 20 times.
// v0.18.3.307: #112 -- recovery must CONVERGE: every recovery re-path now aims at the same goal triangle the drive started with, and revisiting a recently-stuck triangle no longer refills the recovery budget. ONE change (two inseparable halves of "recovery stops undoing itself"). .306 BAT: Aaron reported NO more "Player position lost" (#111 CONFIRMED -- zero occurrences in the log, the drive survived the whole session) but "navigation really struggled to actually get around... stuck on objects, like the rails around the stairs and the short wall around the hole in the middle of the floor". The log splits that experience into two distinct faults. FAULT 1 (pre-existing, #110 territory, NOT touched here): every gpbig1a drive before the 15:11:44 empirical calibration ran on ca-quantized axes that are 90 degrees wrong on that field (the calibration proves it: camRight (1,0)->(0,1)), so the drive pressed keys rotated a quarter turn and ground along the rails/knee wall -- drive 1 ran EAST while the stairs were south-west, drive 4 pressed UL into the same wall for 27 s with moveDist=0 and 20 identical nudges (the #100 signature). Meanwhile the SAME build drove gpbig2a (axes correct at load, flipBand=0) flawlessly through 6 waypoints to the exit on the first post-restart attempt -- same code, adjacent field, the difference is the axes. The .305 cache cannot help the FIRST load of a session because it dies with the process; the real fix stays gated on #110 independent data (gpgmn3/gpbrdg1/gppark1 still unvisited). FAULT 2 (NEW, #112, fixed here): the two post-calibration drives had correct axes and STILL failed -- the final drive ping-ponged between tri 8/9/10 for 40 s, and the goal alternation in the log is the mechanism. For trigger targets UpdateAutoDrive() offsets tx/tz 300 units past the line center along the direction FROM THE PLAYER'S CURRENT POSITION ("aim through the line"); the odd-phase and nudge-fallback recovery re-paths seeded FindNearestTriangle() with that MOVING point, so the A* goal depended on where the player stood: goal=1 (from the west cluster) pulls wp (-2361,-503) WEST, goal=22 (from the east cluster) pulls wp (-1683,69) NORTH-EAST, and each recovery routed the player toward the opposite cluster forever. Drive start and the wiggle-completion re-path both use the raw center and agree on goal=48 -- the recovery block was the odd site out (the "check ALL passes writing the same field" rule, again). FIX A: capture rawTx/rawTz before the offset, seed every recovery re-path with it. FIX B (inseparable, the #100 half): each ping-pong hop changed the walkmesh triangle, and the v0.17.6.1 tri-advance reset treated every hop as corridor progress and refilled MAX_RECOVERY_PHASES -- so "Stuck." was UNREACHABLE and the drive bounced silently until the 40 s timeout. A 4-entry ring of recently-stuck triangles (field_nav_autodrive_helpers.inl, cleared at drive start in handlekeys) now gates the reset: fresh triangles still reset, revisits log "recovery counter NOT reset" and let the phase climb to the cap, so the drive either progresses or honestly says "Stuck. Distance remaining: N" within ~30 recovery windows. Waypoint advances keep their own independent phase reset, so genuine corridor traversal keeps its budget. Nothing lands in field_navigation.cpp (81,792/81,920 -- still the next file to split, #37). BAT: walk a few steps on gpbig1a first so "Camera calibrated." fires, THEN F9 to the stairs -- expect every recovery [A*] line to show the SAME goal, "NOT reset" lines where the old build ping-ponged, and either arrival at the stairs or an honest Stuck within ~40 s. A few steps on gpgmn3/gpbrdg1/gppark1 would finally give #110 its independent verdict.
// v0.18.3.306: #111 -- auto-drive no longer dies on a ONE-FRAME player-position dropout. ONE change. .305 BAT: Aaron got 11 "Player position lost" stops in four minutes and said he has been seeing it more and more, and guessed it was tied to crossing some underlying line on the field. He is essentially right, and the log shows the shape of it exactly: the position is readable on the tick BEFORE and the tick AFTER every single one. 14:51:39 [drive-vec] t=150 tri=3 pp=(-2386,-372) -> next tick "Player position lost." -> 14:51:42 [A*] start=2 fine again. 14:49:21 [drive-vec] t=270 tri=55 pp=(1627,275) -> next tick lost. GetEntityPos() only fails in-range when the triangle id at 0x1FA reads 0 ("not yet placed") or the Others base pointer is momentarily null -- both of which are things the engine does WHILE re-resolving the player walkmesh placement, i.e. exactly when crossing into a new triangle or when a line fires. At 14:51:39 the drive had just advanced THREE waypoints in one second (tri 56 -> 6 -> 3), so it was crossing triangles fast. It is a race between our per-frame poll and the engine placement update, which is why it gets more likely the more auto-drive is used and the faster the player moves -- matching "happening more and more". THE FIX DOES NOT DEPEND ON KNOWING WHICH OF THE TWO READS DIPS: one failed sample is not evidence the player is gone, and this log proves that directly. A dropout now pauses the drive exactly as an open dialog already does (release held keys, freeze the stuck counter, keep the drive alive) and only gives up after 30 consecutive bad ticks (~0.5 s) -- far longer than any observed dropout (all were a SINGLE tick), far shorter than a player would wait wondering what happened. If the position really is gone for good, IsOnField() and the field-transition handler stop the drive on their own paths, so nothing depended on this one being trigger-happy. Also SPLIT THE MESSAGE: s_playerEntityIdx < 0 now says "Player entity unknown." because it and the GetEntityPos failure both said "Player position lost.", so the .305 log could not tell me which fired and I had to reason it out from surrounding lines -- they are different faults. Logs [drive] player position unreadable -- PAUSING with the raw othersBase/tri values once per dropout, and a recovered-after-N-ticks line, so the next BAT CONFIRMS the mechanism rather than leaving it inferred. New ReadPlayerPlacementRaw() lives in field_nav_autodrive_helpers.inl and owns its own SEH: MSVC C2712 forbids __try in a function needing C++ object unwinding and UpdateAutoDrive() has none today, so the SEH stays out of it (same pattern as GetEntityPos). .305 BAT RESULTS, all three confirmed: #109 the axes cache WORKS -- one calibration on gpbig1a then "cached axes re-applied on field load" on FOUR subsequent loads with no relearn, matching Aaron's "it did need to calibrate on the left side, but after that its directions seemed accurate". #110 fired its first verdict and it matches the prediction exactly: gpbig1a rawCA=+38.32 quantizerChose=+0 alternative=+90 measured=+90 -> ALTERNATIVE CORRECT, threshold is below 45. NOTE that is still the field the hypothesis was DERIVED from, so it is consistency, not independent confirmation -- gpgmn3/gpbrdg1/gppark1 were not visited and the threshold change stays unshipped until one of them reports. gpbig2a (flipBand=0) never needed correcting, which is consistent but weak. #107/#108 unchanged and still in place.
// v0.18.3.305: #109 -- the .304 calibration is CORRECT but it is thrown away on every field load, and the prison shaft reloads its field on every floor change. That is the whole of Aaron's "you have to approach the downward stairs in a particular direction and the mod got hung up on it". The log settles it without inference. gpbig1a loaded EIGHT times in four and a half minutes (plus three gpbig2a loads) while the calibration fired only TWICE, and the two lists correlate perfectly: 12:27:14 load -> 12:27:22 calibrated -> the 12:27:28/29/45/51 drives are the ones Aaron said were working; 12:28:10 load -> never recalibrated -> 12:28:44 drive -> 20 identical recovery attempts -> "Gave up. Distance remaining: 999." at 12:28:53; 12:29:07 load (floor 4) -> never recalibrated -> 12:29:08 drive -> wedged AT the stairs, 56 identical position samples. With the axes wrong the drive presses the wrong arrow keys, so whether the player makes progress depends on how the axis error happens to line up with the approach -- it is not the staircase geometry being fussy, it is the steering being rotated. Caught red-handed at 12:29:09: the drive wanted world (-53,-110) (west-south) and with the true mapping (screen-up = world -X, screen-right = world +Y) that is UP-LEFT, but using the stale ca-quantized axes it pressed DOWN-LEFT, which moves world (+X,-Y) -- the opposite in X. Player froze at (-2154,-100), 12 units short of crossing line1, and stayed there. FIX = cache the corrected axes per fieldId and re-apply them at field load (new CamAxesCacheStore/Lookup, 24 entries, round-robin). Placed in field_nav_helpers.inl, NOT field_navigation.cpp: that file is at 81,792 of its 81,920 hard limit and DEVNOTES says split it before landing anything there rather than shaving comments to fit, so I put the new code where there is room instead. helpers.inl is included ahead of both fieldscripts.inl (applies the cache) and observe.inl (fills it). SECOND, INSEPARABLE PART -- the one-shot lock becomes a per-load CAP of 3, and "empirical-cached"/"empirical-corrected" join "ca-quantized" as contradictable sources. This is not scope creep, it is what makes cacheing safe: the .304 BAT applied ONE CORRECTION THAT WAS 180 DEGREES WRONG (12:25:38, arrow=DOWN consensus (-0.872,0.490) = world 151deg -> camRight=(0,-1), versus 12:27:22 arrow=UP consensus (-0.995,0.100) = world 174deg -> camRight=(0,+1)); opposite keys cannot both move the player the same way, so one sample was a wall slide and the DOWN answer was the bad one. Under a hard one-shot lock that wrong answer stood for the rest of the visit, and cacheing it unchanged would have made it stand for the rest of the SESSION. A 180-degree error diverges far past the 45-degree threshold, so it now self-repairs on the next clean sample, and the cap bounds oscillation. ALSO SEEN, NOT FIXED THIS CYCLE (filed, belongs to #100): the wedge recovery repeats an IDENTICAL nudge -- same triangle pair, same direction vector, same 8 ticks -- up to 20 times. tri 10->54 dir=(0.96,0.28) ten times; tri 75->74 dir=(-1.00,0.02) ten times. A deterministic action that failed cannot succeed by repetition, and it burned 38 seconds before giving up. Expected on BAT: [NAV-CAL] cached axes re-applied on the SECOND and later entries to each shaft field, no relearn wait, and the drive behaving on arrival the way it behaved after calibration in .304. ALSO FOLDED IN, LOG-ONLY, ZERO BEHAVIOUR CHANGE -- #110, the CA snap-threshold probe. Aaron asked whether the field data itself could give us the orientation instead of a diagnostic that needs many sessions, so I extracted the .ca camera record for ALL 894 fields straight out of field.fs offline (offline/CAMERA_ANGLES.csv + CAMERA_ANGLE_SURVEY.md; parser validated against the mod's own [CA] log lines -- gpbig1a 38.32, bgryo1_4 46.60, bghall_1 7.73, bg2f_1 65.38, bgroom_1 -62.49, bghall_4 23.80 all match exactly). Two things fell out that the v0.17.5 quantizer was written without. (1) FF8 cameras are NOT near-axis-aligned: only 35% of fields sit within 5 degrees of a cardinal and 44% discard more than 20, so the 90-degree snap is making a large correction across most of the game rather than tidying a rounding error. (2) The engine's threshold is NOT 45 degrees. gpbig1a's measured response gives camRight ~= 90 (RIGHT -> world 84, DOWN -> world -5.7, two independent arrows agreeing) while its raw CA is 38.32 and roundf picks 0; against the fields known to work (bghall_1 7.73, ggroom1 17.55, ggsta1 19.80, bghall_4 23.80 snapping to 0 correctly; bgryo1_4 46.60, bg2f_1 65.38, bgroom_1 -62.49 snapping up correctly) the true threshold brackets to (23.8, 38.32]. I am NOT shipping that threshold change: it would flip 93 fields, and although EVERY field known to work today is outside that band and gpbig1a is inside it -- which is the strongest evidence it is safe -- 93 fields is far too large a blast radius to bet on ONE confirmed data point. So this ships the measurement. [CAM-SNAP] logs the raw CA angle, both snap candidates and a flipBand flag at every field load; [CAM-SNAP-VERDICT] fires when a calibration lands and states whether the engine agreed with the quantizer's pick, with the one it rejected, or with neither. Every field that calibrates anywhere in the game is now a data point, so the answer accrues during ordinary play instead of needing a survey campaign. PREDICTIONS ON RECORD: gpgmn3 (+38.80) and gpbrdg1 (+129.10) should report ALTERNATIVE exactly as gpbig1a does; gppark1 (-46.80) and gpbig2a (-24.76) should report QUANTIZER. If gppark1 or gpbig2a also reports ALTERNATIVE the model is wrong and no threshold change ships. SEPARATE BUG FOUND BY THE SAME SURVEY, filed not fixed: 45 fields carry more than one camera and LoadCameraAxes reads camera 0 only; 18 of them have cameras that snap to DIFFERENT cardinals, so a script-driven camera switch puts the axes a full cardinal out with nothing in the log -- glwitch1, glstage1 and ggroom1 are on that list and all three appear in the sewer/gate work.
// v0.18.3.304: #107 + #108 -- the stairs LIST correctly now but Aaron could not GET to them, and the two reasons are unrelated to each other and to the catalog. TWO changes this cycle, deliberately, flagged: neither is testable without the other (with the false arrival fixed the drive still steers on 50-degree-wrong axes and wanders; with the axes fixed the drive still stops short and says it arrived) and they ride the same walk. Different files, different log tags, so the BAT attributes each independently. #107 FALSE ARRIVAL (field_nav_autodrive.inl): the trigger-line arrival test was the INFINITE-LINE side test -- the FIFTH site of that bug and the first outside a catalog filter (v0.17.8.10 gateways, .268 interactions, .278 exits, #94 events all got bounded; this one was missed). gpbig1a line1 is (-2141,-89)->(-2158,-305): 217 units long and almost parallel to the Y axis, so its infinite extension is a wall across the whole map at X=-2150 and walking to ANY point past that X flips the sign. Log: player started at (-2221,-1089) crossNow=-280, reached (-2162,-1161) crossNow=+13688 -> "Arrived.", keys released, ~960 units from the staircase. In a catalog filter this bug makes a row flicker; here it is a confident spoken claim that the destination has been reached, which a blind player has no way to catch. Fix = keep the sign flip AND require the player to be alongside the segment (project onto the line, foot of projection within it, 15% end margin). Failing the bound is the safe failure: the drive keeps walking and the engine's own MAPJUMP ends it. #108 CAMERA AXES (field_nav_observe.inl): NAV-OBSERVE logged 20+ samples on gpbig1a diverging 48-84 degrees and never converging; gpbig2a the same. Root cause is the 90-degree CA quantizer -- camRight angle +38.3deg, only 6.7deg from the 45deg coin-flip boundary, snapped to 0 and threw away 38 degrees (gpbig2a: -24.8 -> 0). NOT fixing the quantizer: the raw CA does not match the engine either (measured screen-up ~145deg, raw predicts ~128, quantized predicts 90), so "use the raw value" trades one wrong answer for a less wrong one. The real defect is that the closed-loop empirical calibrator built for exactly this was hard-gated to s_camAxesSource=="identity", so a ca-quantized field could NEVER be corrected however badly the engine contradicted it -- it watched all 20+ samples and was forbidden to act. Now a ca-quantized field becomes eligible once the engine contradicts it: triggering sample AND consensus must both diverge >= 45deg. 45 is not tuned, it is the cardinal sector boundary -- below it the spoken cardinal is still right so there is nothing to fix; at or above it the cardinal is provably wrong. REGRESSION SAFETY: the gate measures harm on THIS field rather than guessing which fields look risky -- correct axes produce consensus agreeing with the prediction, so the gate stays shut and nothing is written. I first tried gating on "the quantizer discarded a lot of angle" and rejected it: bgryo1_4 discards 43.4deg and navigates fine, so that test would have fired on a healthy field. Expected: consensus UP ~146deg -> camDown snaps (1,0), camRight (0,1), and the stairs then read RIGHT and slightly DOWN from Aaron's position -- which is what he said they should be, and 90 degrees off the "north" he was given.
// v0.18.3.303: #91 R1 completion -- the DOWN staircase now lists too. .302 shipped the naming and Aaron BAT'd it: "Stairs up to Floor 7" was there and in the right place, the down stairs were not there at all. The log said why, precisely. The STAIRS log line for line2 read higher=0 lower=1 -- my own comparison loop had FOUND line1, read its Z and counted it, so line1 was present, active, SCREEN_BOUND and sharing the destination. It was therefore being dropped further up the block, silently, before the stair path could name it -- and it was still coming out the other side as "Interaction 1". One filter fits all three facts, and the log confirms it rather than my inferring it: '[JSMScan] REQ-interact: Line ent1 squall REQs interactive entity -> hasDialogReqTarget=1', and [LINE-PAIR] maps gpbig1a line1 -> jsm1 'squall'. line2 maps to jsm2 'zell', which has no REQ target. That is the whole asymmetry: the up stairs are a plain walk-across line, the down stairs run a scripted descent WITH DIALOGUE, so the down line legitimately REQs a dialog-bearing entity and got classified dual-purpose. The dual-purpose rule ("if it talks it is an Interaction, not an Exit") is right for a dormitory bed and wrong for a staircase, which is the only way off the floor. Two edits, both narrowly scoped to prison-shaft self-destination lines so beds and every other dual-purpose line behave exactly as before. (1) field_nav_catalog_triglines.inl: hoist the shaft-staircase test ABOVE the hasDialogReqTarget continue, and exempt shaft stairs from it, logging when the exemption fires so the next BAT can see it. (2) field_nav_catalog.inl: the Interaction block has TWO branches that can set isInteractive, and .302 guarded only the second (self-loop); the down stairs took the FIRST (hasDialogReqTarget) and kept emitting "Interaction 1". Now guarded before both. This is the DEVNOTES rule "when a fix targets one of several passes writing the same field, check ALL of them" -- I wrote that rule and then broke it in .302. Expected on BAT: gpbig1a lists BOTH "Stairs down to Floor 5" and "Stairs up to Floor 7" on Floor 6, no "Interaction 1", and two [refresh] STAIRS log lines (line1 higher=1 lower=0 -> down; line2 higher=0 lower=1 -> up).
// v0.18.3.302: #91 R1 -- the prison stairs are finally in the catalog, named by direction and destination floor. ONE change. Aaron's clue closed it: he remembered the game answering "(No sense going back up.)" when he crossed a line trying to go back upstairs, and that line plus the SETLINE geometry identifies both staircases. gpbig1a carries TWO self-destination SCREEN_BOUND lines and BOTH were lost -- line1 surfaced as the meaningless "Interaction 1" and line2 did not surface at all, so the way up and the way down were between them invisible. WHICH IS WHICH: line1 centre (-2150,-197) z=-68/-55 is DOWN; line2 centre (-2276,269) z=+352/+391 is UP. Two independent confirmations: (a) all three floor changes in the .301 BAT fired from (-2400,-470), south of and just past line1, floor decreasing each time; (b) the "(No sense going back up.)" refusal came from the NORTH line, so north is up and it is story-gated at that point in the plot. THE RULE IS THE Z ONE -- of the self-destination lines on a field, the higher-Z one goes up. That is exactly the discriminator I tried on INF gateways in .298 and had to withdraw when their lineZ came back (0,0) everywhere; SETLINE data is the opposite, the heights are real and ~430 units apart here, about one floor. Same idea, correct data source. Deliberately NOT keyed on line index or SYM ('squall'/'zell' here, meaningless) so it carries to the other shaft screens on its own, and direction is only claimed when a counterpart EXISTS to be higher or lower than -- a lone line or a tie gets the unqualified "Stairs" rather than a coin-flip, because announcing "Stairs up" at the down stairs is worse than saying nothing. Also (R4) stairs are EXEMPT from the bounded separation test: gpbig1a's west wall carries five lines within ~500 units, so whichever one the player is not standing next to reads as separated by its neighbours -- which is precisely how line2 vanished while line1 survived. That test has now produced a false positive in FOUR blocks (.17.8.10 gateways, .268 interactions, .278 exits, #94 events) and a cluster of parallel lines on one wall is its worst case; losing the only way off a floor is far worse than listing a staircase you have to walk around to. The Interaction block no longer promotes shaft self-loops, so nothing appears twice; the dormitory bed and every other self-loop field is untouched. New ReadShaftFloor() in field_nav_helpers.inl reads varblock 0x01B5 + 1, same source as the .301 announcement. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: on any shaft floor open the catalog -- expect "Stairs up to Floor N+1" AND "Stairs down to Floor N-1" as two separate entries, plus a [refresh] STAIRS line per staircase showing meanZ and the higher/lower counts. Walk to each and confirm the direction is right; if they are swapped the Z sign convention is inverted and it is a one-line flip. NOTE the up stairs may refuse with "(No sense going back up.)" depending on story state -- that is the game talking, not the label being wrong. Prior: v0.18.3.301: THREE separable units, all serving the same walk so one BAT exercises all of them; each carries its own log tag so a regression stays attributable. Aaron described the shaft geometry and it reframed everything: gpbig1a/gpbig2a are the LEFT and RIGHT halves of ONE circular walkway around a central hole, joined by a crossing at the TOP of the circle and another at the BOTTOM. The INF data corroborates exactly -- each half carries two gateways at mirrored heights (y ~= +1800 / -1850) -- and it explains why 10 crossings produced 0 floor changes. (1) #90 FLOOR ANNOUNCEMENT, the headline fix: the shaft changes floor WITHOUT changing fieldId so FieldAnnounce's fieldId-change trigger never fired and 23 floor changes announced NOTHING; the announce key is now (fieldId, floor) and the floor comes from varblock 0x01B5 + 1 (the +1 pinned by the .299 auto-capture -- "Floor 6" while the varblock held 5, "Floor 4" while it held 3). The ring halves DROP their archive ordinal entirely and speak "Floor 4, left side" / "Floor 4, right side" -- "Galbadia D-District Prison 3" is neither a floor nor a place and Aaron heard it alternating with "...5" thirteen times during a three-floor descent. Other shaft fields keep their name and gain ", Floor N". (2) #91 R3 GATEWAY SPLIT + ring crossing names: grouping on destFieldId alone collapsed two exits 3,644 units apart into one entry at their midpoint -- open floor belonging to neither, which auto-drive walked to. A candidate now joins a group only if also within 800 units of it (genuine duplicates are < 200 apart). Both then need distinct names or the .270 exact-name dedupe drops one, so they become "Top crossing" / "Bottom crossing" -- position on the ring, which is the only fact that distinguishes them, since both land on the SAME floor and a floor label would be actively wrong. Derived from the gateway's own Y, not the camera, so it is stable wherever the player stands. ASSUMPTION FLAGGED: larger Y = top. The codebase says "Y = screen-vertical" but never documents the SIGN, and I am not repeating the .298 gateway-Z mistake of assuming one -- both centres are logged and a swap is a one-line flip. (3) #91 R1 groundwork, DIAGNOSTIC ONLY: [MAPJUMP-HOOK] now logs player XY at fire time. gpbig1a has TWO self-destination lines ~470 units apart on the west wall (line1 (-2150,-197), line2 (-2275,269)); one is up and one is down and NOTHING static separates them -- gateway Z is (0,0) everywhere and the nav_data trail only records triangle changes so it went quiet 13-49 s before each jump and never caught the crossing. Position at the fire, next to the floor the jump produces, pins it in one ordinary descent. DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS BUILD: naming the stairs. Surfacing them needs R1 plus the reachability drop that swallowed line2 (R4), and until the diagnostic says which is which they would both read "Stairs" with no way to choose -- so they ship next cycle, labelled "Stairs to Floor N" as Aaron asked. BAT: walk the shaft with -/= on BOTH halves. Expect "Floor N, left side"/"right side" on every floor change INCLUDING the same-fieldId ones, two crossings instead of one merged phantom, [refresh] INF-GW gw SPLIT and ring crossings named lines, and the first-ever GATEWAY grp rows in the dump (#92, still never executed). TELL ME IF TOP/BOTTOM ARE SWAPPED. Prior: v0.18.3.300: FIRST BEHAVIOUR CHANGE of this arc -- #91 R2, the stale-gateway rule is scoped back to its evidence base. ONE change. The .299 BAT caught that rule deleting a REAL exit: on gpbig2a (Prison 5) the catalog held exactly TWO entries on all five visits -- a cell door and the Directory -- and no listed way back to the rest of the floor, which Aaron then walked through anyway 30 seconds later, proving it live. The premise is inverted there: gpbig2a has ONE screen-bound line and it points at a CELL (965); that single unrelated line arms fieldHasLineExits, which then deletes the only real exit off the screen. The shaft screens use BOTH mechanisms for DIFFERENT exits, which the rule never contemplated. Fix: the rule now also requires s_gatewayCount <= 1, i.e. the single-gateway shape it was actually validated on. Re-reading its own evidence block: it was derived from and has only ever been needed by glfurin1, which carries ONE gateway; every other field listed (glpreo1/2/3, glprefr2, glstage1) resolves zero line exits so fieldHasLineExits is false and it never fires there; glwitch1 has both but its gateway dest EQUALS its line dest so it is kept by the agreement test either way. Provably inert on the whole documented evidence base, preserves glfurin1, fixes both prison screens (INF parsed: 2 active gateways on gpbig1a AND gpbig2a). MUST be s_gatewayCount (raw) not s_dedupGatewayCount -- the prison's two gateways share a destination and merge into ONE group, so the dedup count is 1 there and would gate nothing. This is the same move .291 made when it rescoped the addr-as-literal heuristic to bg* after it fabricated a Centra Ruins exit. DIRECTION OF RISK: a pure narrowing -- it can only ADD exits, never remove one; worst case is a ghost gateway reappearing on some unvisited multi-gateway field, versus losing a real exit which is this project's worst failure mode (#88, ladline7, gpbig2a). Logs a [refresh] stale-gateway rule NOT applied line when the gate engages, so the BAT can see it. Also settled by the .299 auto-capture and now closed: the displayed prison floor is the DIALOG SLOT FIELD_VAR_TABLE_BASE[0x20], read straight off two screenshots ("Floor 6" with slot=6/vb=5, "Floor 4" with slot=4/vb=3), so varblock 0x01B5 is floor MINUS ONE -- #89/#90 both have their answer and the announcement needs vb+1. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: walk the shaft with -/= pressed on BOTH screens. Expect gpbig2a to gain 'Exit to Galbadia D-District Prison 3' (2 entries -> 3) and gpbig1a to gain 'Exit to Prison 5', a [refresh] stale-gateway rule NOT applied line on each, and the first-ever GATEWAY grp row in the catalog dump (#92, still unexercised because this rule was deleting every gateway before the dump could print it) -- which should read merged=2 and is also the first direct evidence of R3. Prior: v0.18.3.299: DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY, one addition on top of .298 -- [FLOOR-SHOT], the mod takes the screenshot itself. The .298 BAT found the prison floor number in two places that disagree by exactly one: the engine display slot FIELD_VAR_TABLE_BASE[0x20] read 6->5->4 while field varblock 0x01B5 (437) read 4->3->2, stepping together on every MAPJUMP3 self-jump. Only one is the number printed on screen and picking wrong makes every future floor announcement off by one. The obvious settler -- F11 while the Floor window is up -- is exactly what this bug makes impossible: the window renders 190+ times per floor but decodes to the bare string "Floor" every time, so the dedup eats every repeat and Aaron heard it ONCE for a three-floor descent. He cannot hear when it is on screen, so he cannot time a keypress to it; asking him to was my mistake. FloorShotProbe() now fires from the show_dialog text path, matching the RAW FF8 bytes 4A 6A 6D 6D 70 20 ("Floor ") so no decode is needed, placed BEFORE the hash and decoded dedups because the raw buffer is byte-identical on every floor (the engine substitutes the digits at render time; they are never in the text). Rate-limited on the VALUE not the frame -- one capture per distinct (slot, varblock) pair, i.e. exactly once per floor -- and BOTH values go in the filename (floor_HHMMSS_mmm_s20-5_vb-4.png) so the correlation is readable straight off the directory listing. Async capture with a 30-frame delay, the same mechanism the Scan UI uses, because this runs on the game thread inside a MinHook callback where the blocking variant would stall the renderer. Gated to prison fieldIds (0x0319-0x032E, 0x03C5) and behind FLOOR_SHOT_PROBE, inert everywhere else. Also adds SafeReadByte() alongside the existing SafeReadDword(). g++ compile-clean and behaviour-tested on the extracted function: fires on a Floor window, dedupes repeats, ignores non-Floor windows, re-fires when the value changes, ignores non-prison fields. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: walk any prison shaft floors; expect one floor_*.png per floor plus a [FLOOR-SHOT] line naming both candidates. ALSO STILL NEEDED from .298: press -/= once on a shaft screen -- the catalog was never opened last run so the #92 dump fix and #91's R1/R2/R3 are still untested. Prior: v0.18.3.298: DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY build, zero behaviour change by design -- the two read-only probes from the 2026-07-31 BAT review, combined because both are pure observation and neither can influence the other. (1) [VAR-DROP] (#89): the D-District Prison floor indicator is "Floor " + a 0x04 numeric insert with param 0x30, and FieldExpandRawVars drops any param outside [0x20..0x27] -- so Aaron heard a bare "Floor" on all six occurrences, and the same branch silently eats the quantity at field draw points ("Laguna stocked  Curagas"). Widening the range blind is NOT safe: if params >= 0x28 resolve through a different table we would splice an unrelated number into the sentence, and a confidently wrong floor is worse than no floor. So this logs the dropped param, the dword the engine WOULD have read at 0x01D2B4B0 + param*4, and a sweep of table[0x20..0x3F]; throttled to one line per param per 2 s. (2) [FLOOR-PROBE] (#90): the shaft changes floor WITHOUT changing fieldId -- 23 of the BAT's transitions were 795 -> 795, and FieldAnnounce bails on curId == lastAnnounced, so those floor changes announced nothing at all. Added a prison-gated (0x0319-0x032E, 0x03C5) byte dump of varblock 0x0190-0x01CF at MAPJUMP fire time, covering the 0x01A5 SET3 anchor and the 0x01B4/0x01B5 state pair; the existing 0x80-0xFE word window already covers 0x00C8/0x00D4 for diffing. (3) [INF-GW-Z] (#91): LoadINFGateways read both exit-line Z values and threw them away as "not used for 2D nav" and never parsed destinationPoint at +12 at all. True for navigation, false for IDENTIFICATION: on the shaft, up and down from gpbig1a BOTH land in gpbig2a, so destination fieldId cannot separate them and the catalog merges them into one entry at a midpoint belonging to neither staircase. Trigger lines already keep their Z and the two gpbig1a stairs read z ~= -61 and z ~= +371 there, stable across all 15 visits. Now stored on GatewayInfo (lineZ1/lineZ2/destX/destY/destZ) and logged on a SEPARATE line so the existing [INF-GW] line stays byte-comparable against every prior BAT log. Nothing consumes it yet -- whether +Z means up in field walkmesh space is NOT established (DEVNOTES records UP = neg-Y for wmx.obj, which does not transfer) and the BAT settles the sign, it is not assumed. (4) [refresh] catalog dump fix (#92): the dump tested entityIdx <= -300 before anything looked at -400, so every INF gateway exit fell into the JSM branch, computed slot 100 + d, failed the bounds check and printed NOTHING -- silently. Every